@* An ⓘ button that opens an explanation of one part of the page: what it shows, how it is measured and
   how to read it. The browser's Popover API opens and closes it (Esc, a click outside or ×), so it
   needs no script and no round trip, and stays open while Blazor redraws the page every second. *@

<button type="button" class="help-button" popovertarget="@Id" aria-label="About @Title" title="What this shows">i</button>
<div id="@Id" popover class="help" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="@(Id)-title">
    <div class="help-head">
        <h3 id="@(Id)-title">@Title</h3>
        <button type="button" class="help-close" popovertarget="@Id" popovertargetaction="hide" aria-label="Close">×</button>
    </div>
    @switch (Topic)
    {
        case HelpTopic.Rate:
            <p>
                How many requests per second the load generator starts: 0 to 400 on the slider. Browsing and holds both count.
                A hold and its release count once here, though they are two HTTP requests.
            </p>
            <h4>How it is sent</h4>
            <p>
                The generator follows a fixed schedule (an <em>open model</em>, ADR-0017). After you set the rate, request
                <em>n</em> is due at <em>n</em> ÷ rate seconds, whether or not earlier answers came back. Real fans don't wait
                for each other either. With a fixed number of users who each wait for their answer, the load would slow down
                together with the system and hide the overload.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Raise the rate in steps and wait about 20 s at each. Whichever of these happens first marks this machine's
                    capacity for this mix: <strong>Achieved</strong> falls behind what was sent, <strong>In flight</strong> keeps
                    climbing, a <strong>p99</strong> passes its target, or <strong>Failed</strong> goes above 0.
                </li>
                <li>On the owner's machine, one instance of each host carried about 200–250 requests/s of the default mix (20% holds).</li>
                <li>
                    There is one generator. Every open lab page shows and changes the same settings, and closing the page
                    doesn't stop the load: press <strong>Stop</strong>.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Holds:
            <p>The share of the scheduled requests that are holds. The rest browse.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>A hold:</strong> a lab fan (<code>lab01</code> to <code>lab200</code>) asks PublicApi for the best free
                    place in a section and lets go of it straight away. That is two requests: the hold
                    (<code>POST /events/{id}/holds</code>) and the release (<code>DELETE /holds/{id}</code>). Nothing is sold, so
                    the places keep cycling. The fan signs in at Keycloak before the hold, and that time isn't counted.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Browsing:</strong> anonymous. Each request is one of three, picked at random: the event list, the
                    event page or availability.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Holds write to the database, and browsing only reads. The more holds in the mix, the lower the rate the system
                    can carry.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>The generator's limit:</strong> a fan has at most one hold at a time (one hold per fan per event,
                    ADR-0024). Two hundred fans manage about 200 ÷ (hold time + release time) holds a second: over 1,000 while
                    each takes 60–70 ms, more than PublicApi serves here. When holds slow down, the fans stay busy for longer
                    and the limit falls: at a second for a hold and its release, it is 200 a second. Holds beyond the limit are
                    <em>skipped</em> (see Offered).
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Event:
            <p>
                The on-sale event that gets the load. <em>The first event on sale</em> is the first one in PublicApi's list, Demo
                Concert after seeding. The <strong>Free places</strong> panel follows the event chosen here.
            </p>
            <p>
                Choosing another event resets <strong>Holds go for</strong>, because each event has its own sections. Nothing on
                sale? Run the seeder: <code>dotnet run --project tooling/seed</code>. In the lab cluster, <code>lab up</code> and
                <code>lab reset</code> seed it for you.
            </p>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Section:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Any section:</strong> each hold picks one of the event's sections at random. Fans spread out and
                    rarely compete.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>One section only:</strong> every hold goes to the same section, a <em>hot section</em>, as at an
                    on-sale. All fans want the same best places.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Inventory claims places in a single SQL statement that skips any place another fan is claiming at that moment
                    (ADR-0011). So nobody waits on a lock: a fan who loses a race gets the next-best place, or a 409 straight away
                    if none is left. Expect more <strong>Refused</strong> answers and the same latency.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Failed</strong> must stay at 0. One place never goes to two fans at once, and the T2 concurrency test
                    proves that in CI.
                </li>
                <li>With <strong>Holds</strong> at 100% and one section, this is the lab's contention test.</li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Scenarios:
            <p>
                Scripts that move the <strong>Rate</strong> for you. Holds, Event and Section stay as you set them. Hover over a
                button to see its steps.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Steady 20/s:</strong> a quiet baseline. Everything should be flat, with no failures.</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Step 50 → 150:</strong> 30 s at 50, then 150. This is the step T11 measures: it needs three times
                    the instances, and an autoscaler must have them ready within 60 s.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>On-sale spike:</strong> 20 → 200 within 10 s, held for 30 s, then back to 20. It arrives faster than
                    any autoscaler could react.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Past capacity:</strong> 400 for 20 s, then back to 20. It shows overload and the way back: T7 asks for
                    normal again within 60 s. Run it last.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="muted">
                A scenario runs in this page. Moving the slider or pressing <strong>Stop</strong> ends it. If you close the page,
                the generator stays at the rate of the step it was on.
            </p>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Offered:
            <p>
                The requests per second the schedule asked for, which is the <strong>Rate</strong> slider, averaged over the last
                5 complete seconds.
            </p>
            <h4>Skipped</h4>
            <p>Asked for, but never sent, because the generator held back:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>every one of the 200 lab fans was busy with a hold (see Holds);</li>
                <li>
                    500 requests were already waiting for an answer. The system is far past overload, and the generator protects
                    itself;
                </li>
                <li>nothing was on sale yet.</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Sent</strong> = Offered − skipped. That is what PublicApi actually received, and what
                    <strong>Achieved</strong> is compared with.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Any skipped requests (▲) mean the test isn't what you asked for: the generator sent less. Then look at
                    latency. If it is normal, the generator is the limit. If it is climbing, the system's slowness kept the fans
                    or the 500 places in flight busy, and the skipping is a symptom of overload.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Example: 400/s with 90% holds asks for about 40 browsing requests and 360 holds a second. The 200 fans can
                    send that while a hold and its release take under half a second together. If they take two seconds, the fans
                    manage only about 100 holds a second, and the rest are skipped.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Achieved:
            <p>
                The scheduled requests (browsing and holds, not the releases after them) that finished each second, averaged over
                the last 5 seconds. Every outcome counts: OK, refused or failed.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Compare it with what was <strong>sent</strong>, Offered minus skipped. If they are about equal, the system keeps
                    up.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Below 90% of what was sent (▲), requests are queueing somewhere: in PublicApi, in its database connection pool,
                    or in the database. <strong>In flight</strong> and latency climb at the same time.
                </li>
                <li>
                    After a slow patch it can briefly go above what was sent, because queued answers arrive together.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Failures count as finished, so a system that fails fast "keeps up". Always read this tile together with
                    <strong>Failed</strong>.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Ok:
            <p>
                HTTP answers with a 2xx status each second, averaged over the last 5 seconds. Unlike Achieved, this counts every
                request: browsing, holds <em>and</em> the releases after them.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Why it can be higher than Achieved:</strong> each hold brings a release. When everything succeeds,
                    OK ≈ browsing + 2 × holds. For example, 40 browsing requests and 111 holds a second give about 40 + 111 + 111 =
                    262 OK, while Achieved shows 151.
                </li>
                <li>OK, Refused and Failed together are every answer the generator got.</li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Refused:
            <p>
                Answers each second with <strong>409 Conflict</strong>, <strong>429 Too Many Requests</strong>, or
                <strong>503</strong> with a Retry-After, averaged over the last 5 seconds. These are the system saying no on
                purpose, so they are correct behaviour, not errors.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>409:</strong> the place was just taken by another fan (common in a hot section), or the fan already
                    holds a place at this event. That second case follows an overload: a release that timed out leaves its hold
                    until the hold expires after 8 minutes (1 minute in demo mode).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>429:</strong> a rate or capacity limit. Nothing sets one in the lab yet. M5's capacity settings will
                    turn excess load away quickly instead of letting it queue (T7).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>503 with Retry-After:</strong> "not now, come back in 30 s". An operator has switched on the
                    <code>PauseHolds</code> flag (<code>dotnet run lab.cs -- flag PauseHolds on</code>): new holds are refused
                    while browsing goes on. A 503 without Retry-After is a failure.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>Under contention, refusals with no failures are exactly what you want to see.</p>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Failed:
            <p>
                Requests each second that the system couldn't serve, averaged over the last 5 seconds: 5xx answers, timeouts (no
                answer within 5 s), and broken or refused connections. Any other 4xx counts here too, and the note says so: that
                means a bug in the generator or a change in PublicApi's contract.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    It should be 0 at any rate the system can carry, and also while instances are added or removed and during a
                    rolling deploy (T10: under 0.1%). The tile turns red (⚠) if anything failed in the last 5 seconds.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The first failures under overload are usually timeouts: requests that waited in a queue for more than 5 s.
                </li>
                <li>
                    To find out why, in the lab cluster open Grafana at <code>http://localhost:3000</code>: the
                    <strong>Services</strong> dashboard shows PublicApi's errors and warnings, and Explore → Tempo finds the failed
                    requests' traces (status = error), each with its logs. Under Aspire, open <strong>Traces</strong> in the
                    dashboard the AppHost prints and filter by error.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.HoldP99:
            <p>
                The slowest 1% of the answered holds (OK or refused) in the latest second: 99% were faster than this. It is
                measured by the generator, from sending a request to getting its answer, through Traefik, PublicApi and the
                database. The fan's sign-in isn't included.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>The target is T1: under 200 ms. Above it, the tile shows ▲.</li>
                <li>It shows the 99th percentile rather than the average, because an average hides the slow tail that fans feel.</li>
                <li>
                    At low rates it jumps around. At 20/s with 20% holds there are only 4 holds a second, so the p99 is simply the
                    slowest of 4.
                </li>
                <li>Timeouts have no latency, so they aren't in it. Look at <strong>Failed</strong> for those.</li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.BrowseP99:
            <p>
                The slowest 1% of the answered browsing requests in the latest second: the event list, the event page and
                availability. It is measured the same way as Hold p99.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>The target is T4: under 100 ms. Above it, the tile shows ▲.</li>
                <li>
                    Browsing only reads. If it gets slower as you add holds, reads and writes are competing for the same CPU and
                    database. M3 adds a cache and read replicas to take reads off the primary database.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.InFlight:
            <p>
                The requests sent and not yet finished, at this moment. Unlike the other tiles, it isn't an average.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    In flight ≈ throughput × latency (Little's law). At 150 requests/s and about 100 ms each, that is about 15.
                </li>
                <li>Steady and close to that product: fine.</li>
                <li>
                    Rising every second: requests are queueing, and the system finishes fewer than it is sent. It will reach the
                    generator's limit of 500, then requests are skipped and p99 approaches the 5 s timeout.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Holds can't push it past 200: with one hold per lab fan, at most 200 hold-and-release pairs are in flight.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Instances:
            <p>
                Every host of the product as Kubernetes runs it, read from the Kubernetes API every second.
                <strong>R of W ready</strong>: W instances wanted, R of them ready. Each chip is one instance (a pod):
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>✓ ready:</strong> it passes its readiness check (<code>/health</code>, which includes the database),
                    and Traefik sends it requests.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>▲ starting:</strong> not ready (yet). It has just started, or it is failing its readiness check under
                    load and has been taken out of rotation.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>✕ stopping:</strong> its shutdown has begun. It keeps answering for 5 s while Traefik notices, then
                    finishes its requests and stops.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>↻N:</strong> the container restarted N times, after a crash or a failed liveness check. Hover over a
                    chip to see its full name.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <h4>− and +</h4>
            <p>
                These set PublicApi's count, from 1 to 8, as <code>kubectl scale</code> would, and Kubernetes starts or stops
                instances to match. The limit is 8 because of the database's connection budget: each instance may hold 20
                connections, and the database allows 300 (ADR-0019). With <strong>Auto</strong> on, the autoscaler sets the
                count instead, and − and + are disabled.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    After +, a new chip shows ▲ and turns ✓ within seconds. Failed stays at 0, both when adding instances and when
                    removing them.
                </li>
                <li>
                    On one laptop, more instances share the same CPU and one database. They show how scaling works rather than
                    adding much capacity.
                </li>
                <li>
                    This panel appears only in the lab cluster (<code>dotnet run lab.cs -- up</code>). Under Aspire every host runs
                    once.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.Autoscaling:
            <p>
                With <strong>Auto</strong> on, KEDA's autoscaler sets PublicApi's count, between 1 and 8 (ADR-0054). It reads
                from Prometheus how many requests PublicApi has <strong>in progress</strong>, all instances together, and aims
                for 0.5 per instance (D56):
            </p>
            <p class="formula">wanted = ⌈ requests in progress ÷ 0.5 ⌉, between the minimum and the maximum</p>
            <p>
                Requests in progress is the seconds of request time PublicApi spends per second, over the last 30 s: 100
                requests a second taking 10 ms each are 1 request in progress on average (Little's law). 0.5 is what each of
                three instances had at 50 requests/s. The instances share one laptop, which carries about 400 requests/s: at
                50 each, the maximum of 8 fits it. So 150 requests/s want about 4 instances. Within 10% of the target it
                changes nothing, so the count doesn't twitch.
            </p>
            <h4>Why this and not CPU</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Requests in progress rise whenever requests wait, for the CPU, the database or a lock, and they rise
                    steeply as an instance nears its capacity. CPU only shows the first of those.
                </li>
                <li>
                    It doesn't depend on the machine: CI's slower runners need more instances for the same load, and the
                    measurement says so by itself.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A new instance's warm-up isn't counted (ServiceDefaults leaves it out of the metrics), and Kubernetes'
                    probes aren't either.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <h4>How fast</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The hosts send their metrics every 5 s, Prometheus scrapes them every 5 s, the measurement averages 30 s,
                    and the autoscaler looks every 15 s. So the count moves 20–50 s after the load does, and a new instance
                    needs about 20 s more to warm up and become ready. Part of "scaling is slow" is "measuring is slow"
                    (ADR-0019).
                </li>
                <li>
                    It adds instances once it has wanted them for 20 s, so a short burst doesn't buy them (D43).
                </li>
                <li>
                    It gives them back only after the load has stayed lower for a while. <strong>Demo speed</strong> waits 30 s,
                    <strong>Realistic</strong> 5 minutes, Kubernetes' default, so that a short dip doesn't remove instances
                    needed again a minute later.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Try <strong>Step 50 → 150</strong> with Auto on: the step needs about three times the instances, and T11 asks
                    for them to be ready within 60 s. Then press Stop and watch them go after the wait. Failed should stay at 0
                    both ways.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Requests in progress include waiting for the database. Holds on one section wait for each other's locks,
                    and that wait grows with the total load, not with one instance's share: at 150 requests/s there are
                    about 1.6 in progress, where 50 requests/s on one instance had 0.35. More instances can't remove that
                    wait. When the database is slow, the autoscaler adds instances that can't help, which is why every
                    autoscaler has a maximum (ADR-0019), and what the <em>slow database</em> scenario will show.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Off is KEDA's pause: the count stays where it was, and − and + work again. Every open lab page shows the
                    same switch, because it lives in the cluster. A deploy keeps it as it is.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.RequestsChart:
            <p>
                The last 2 minutes, one point per second: <strong>Offered</strong> (dashed) is what the schedule asked for, and
                <strong>Achieved</strong> is the scheduled requests that finished. Hover to see a second's values.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>The two lines on top of each other: the load is carried.</li>
                <li>
                    Achieved below Offered: if the <strong>Offered</strong> tile shows skipped requests, the gap is the
                    generator's limit. Otherwise the system is saturated.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A spike in Achieved just after a dip: queued requests being answered all at once.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.OutcomesChart:
            <p>
                Every answer of the last 2 minutes by outcome, one point per second: <strong>OK</strong> (2xx),
                <strong>Refused</strong> (409, 429) and <strong>Failed</strong> (5xx, timeouts, broken connections, other 4xx).
                Releases are included, so OK is higher than Achieved whenever there are holds.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Failed should stay flat at zero, through rate changes, scaling and deploys.</li>
                <li>Refused rises with contention (one hot section). That is expected.</li>
                <li>OK flattening while the rate still rises: the system's ceiling for this mix.</li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.LatencyChart:
            <p>
                How long answered requests took, per second, over the last 2 minutes. <strong>p50</strong> is the typical
                request, <strong>p95</strong> and <strong>p99</strong> the slow tail. Browsing, holds and releases are mixed here;
                the p99 tiles show holds and browsing separately.
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Flat lines close together: healthy.</li>
                <li>
                    The p99 pulling away from the p50 is the first sign of queueing. A few requests are waiting, and the typical
                    one isn't yet.
                </li>
                <li>All three climbing: the queue is everyone's.</li>
                <li>A p99 near 5,000 ms: requests close to the generator's timeout. Expect Failed next.</li>
            </ul>
            break;

        case HelpTopic.FreePlaces:
            <p>
                The availability of the event under load, per section (free / total), as fans see it. It is read from PublicApi
                every 2 seconds. Availability is rebuilt about once a second, so it can lag a little (ADR-0011).
            </p>
            <h4>How to read it</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Holds are let go of straight away, so the bars stay near full. The load cycles through the places and nothing
                    is sold.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Places that stay missing after an overload are holds whose release timed out. They expire after 8 minutes (1
                    minute in demo mode). Until then those lab fans get 409s.
                </li>
            </ul>
            break;
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public HelpTopic Topic { get; set; }

    private string Id => $"help-{Topic.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()}";

    private string Title => Topic switch
    {
        HelpTopic.Rate => "Rate",
        HelpTopic.Holds => "Holds",
        HelpTopic.Event => "Event",
        HelpTopic.Section => "Holds go for",
        HelpTopic.Scenarios => "Scenarios",
        HelpTopic.Offered => "Offered",
        HelpTopic.Achieved => "Achieved",
        HelpTopic.Ok => "OK",
        HelpTopic.Refused => "Refused",
        HelpTopic.Failed => "Failed",
        HelpTopic.HoldP99 => "Hold p99",
        HelpTopic.BrowseP99 => "Browse p99",
        HelpTopic.InFlight => "In flight",
        HelpTopic.Instances => "Instances",
        HelpTopic.Autoscaling => "Auto",
        HelpTopic.RequestsChart => "Requests per second",
        HelpTopic.OutcomesChart => "Answers per second, by outcome",
        HelpTopic.LatencyChart => "Latency",
        HelpTopic.FreePlaces => "Free places",
        _ => Topic.ToString(),
    };
}
